course: dictionary
lesson: 14440
title: Palatable: nice to eat, or easy to accept
level: primary-5
stars: 0
explain: Palatable means nice enough to eat or drink. It is about taste, and it does not have to be amazing. Food can be palatable even when it is only okay. «The soup was not exciting, but it was palatable and warm.» Here, the soup tasted fine, so it was palatable.
explain: Palatable can also mean easy to accept. «The doctor made the bitter medicine more palatable by stirring in honey.» Honey made it nicer to take. In a meeting, «The team found the second plan more palatable than the first.» Here, people could accept it more easily.
explain: The opposite is unpalatable. It means nasty to eat, or hard to accept. «The sprouts were so burnt that nobody found them palatable.» Ask yourself: is this food nice enough to eat, or is this idea easy to agree with? If not, it is unpalatable.
fact: Palatable means nice enough to eat or drink, with a pleasant taste.
fact: Palatable can also mean acceptable, or easy to agree with, as in «a more palatable idea».
fact: «Palatable» means nice enough, not perfect. A palatable meal is good enough to enjoy.
fact: «Unpalatable» is the opposite: it means nasty to eat or hard to accept.
fact: A good check is to swap in the word «acceptable» for «palatable» and see if the sentence still makes sense.
exercise: «The stew was not delicious, but it was palatable enough to finish.» What does palatable mean here? | Far too salty to swallow | Left over from yesterday | Very spicy and hot | *Nice enough to eat
exercise: «Everyone agreed the second plan was more palatable.» What does more palatable mean? | *Easier for people to accept | Bigger, with more pages | Louder and more noisy | Older and more worn
exercise: Find the word that means nasty to eat or hard to accept. | pleasant | portable | *unpalatable | palette
test: «The fish tasted so strange that Ben could not find it palatable.» What does this tell you? | Ben asked for it to be cooked again | Ben loved it very much | *Ben did not think it was nice enough to eat | Ben found it on his plate
test: Nadia says palatable is just another word for tasty. Why might an author choose palatable? | It means exactly the same as tasty, so it is never needed | It is the opposite of hungry | *Because it can mean acceptable as well as tasty | It means very loud
exam: «The sweet medicine was more palatable than the bitter old kind.» Which word means the opposite of palatable? | Sweet | Pleasant | *Unpalatable | Tasty
